WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.149
Video games are all about the challenge.

00:00:03.149 --> 00:00:07.790
There's nothing more satisfying than beating
your friend's lap time in Mario Kart or going

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:09.750
on a rampage in Halo.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.809
But if you think you're at the top of your
game, think again.

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:17.560
These guys took dedication to new heights
to set video game records that will probably

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.570
never, ever be broken.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:22.060
Longest Halo marathon

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:27.000
Halo was one of the top launch titles for
the original Xbox console, and continued on

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.390
to spawn one of the top-selling franchises
of all time.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:34.780
And while plenty of Halo records have been
set — and subsequently broken — one record

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:36.420
stands alone.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:42.911
Ending on October 27, 2015, Finnish gamer
Paavo Niskala played Halo 1 through 3 on the

00:00:42.911 --> 00:00:49.100
Heroic setting, Halo 4 and 5 on Legendary,
and then Halo 5 multiplayer, bringing his

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:54.230
total playing time to a whopping 50 hours,
4 minutes, and 17 seconds.

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:57.340
That guy's got thumbs of steel.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:59.969
Longest Super Smash Bros. tournament streak

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:04.920
Nobody's gonna deny that Super Smash Bros.
is a barrel of fun, even the players who never

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.049
could stop falling off that damn edge.

00:01:07.049 --> 00:01:09.729
But some people take things to the next level.

00:01:09.729 --> 00:01:14.609
One of those guys is Gonzalo "ZeRo" Barrios,
who set the record for the longest tournament

00:01:14.609 --> 00:01:17.020
winning streak in Super Smash Bros.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:23.399
Playing on the Wii U, Barrios won 53 consecutive
tournaments between November 2014 and October

00:01:23.399 --> 00:01:24.590
2015.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:29.149
But even Barrios doesn't hold the record for
most tournament wins — that honor belongs

00:01:29.149 --> 00:01:34.669
to Jason Zimmerman, who's won 339 eSports
tournaments in his professional gaming career

00:01:34.669 --> 00:01:39.890
and also appears in the Guinness World Records
2017 Gamer's Edition.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.039
World's largest arcade machine

00:01:42.039 --> 00:01:47.280
Years before console gaming, there was the
arcade — and an entire culture of arcade

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:48.280
gamers.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.670
People may not go to arcades as much as they
used to, but that doesn't mean companies have

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:54.109
stopped manufacturing arcade machines.

00:01:54.109 --> 00:02:00.279
And in 2015, 44-year-old network engineer
Jason Camberis from Chicago, Illinois, not

00:02:00.279 --> 00:02:04.509
only built his own arcade machine — he built
the world's largest.

00:02:04.509 --> 00:02:10.360
The game measures 4.41 meters tall and 1.06
meters deep, which is larger than a fully

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.819
grown African elephant, according to Guinness
World Records.

00:02:13.819 --> 00:02:17.920
It also has the ability to play over 200 classic
arcade games.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.120
Why would anyone build this monstrosity?

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:25.620
"It brings you back in time when you were
young and you were a lot shorter and you felt

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:30.079
that full experience of being in front of
a video game, where you're looking up…"

00:02:30.079 --> 00:02:31.079
Ah, okay.

00:02:31.079 --> 00:02:33.409
We can respect that.

00:02:33.409 --> 00:02:35.650
Highest score on NES Tetris

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:39.049
Almost everyone has played Tetris at least
once in their lifetime.

00:02:39.049 --> 00:02:43.069
But how many of those people are ultimate
Tetris Masters?

00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:48.010
While scoring 10,000 points qualifies you
as a regular, non-ultimate Tetris Master,

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:55.129
only a handful of people have ever reached
the game's max score of 999,999 points on

00:02:55.129 --> 00:02:56.609
the NES game.

00:02:56.609 --> 00:03:01.049
Harry Hong became the first person in the
world to hit that top score, and he reached

00:03:01.049 --> 00:03:02.810
it on Level 28.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:07.250
His record astonished the Tetris community
so much that someone made a documentary about

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:08.689
Hong's achievement.

00:03:08.689 --> 00:03:11.700
Unfortunately, Hong's record would be short-lived.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:19.769
In January 2012, New York gamer Matthew Buco
beat Hong's record by reaching 999,999 points

00:03:19.769 --> 00:03:22.349
by Level 26.

00:03:22.349 --> 00:03:24.299
"...yeahhh!"

00:03:24.299 --> 00:03:28.109
It took him awhile — more than seven months
— but he finally did it.

00:03:28.109 --> 00:03:31.720
And so far, as of the making of this video,
his record stands.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.380
Sounds like he's been...Tetrisized!

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:36.880
"Put a piece here, put a piece there!

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:37.880
Use your thumbs, use your eyes.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:39.140
Find yourself Tetrisized!"

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:42.380
Youngest professional video game player

00:03:42.389 --> 00:03:47.829
In 2005, Victor De Leon III became the youngest
professional videogamer at age seven when

00:03:47.829 --> 00:03:52.409
he signed an exclusive deal with the Major
League Gaming organization...and he goes by

00:03:52.409 --> 00:03:55.269
the gaming name of "LiL Poison."

00:03:55.269 --> 00:03:56.379
Because of course he does.

00:03:56.379 --> 00:03:59.920
"So you're like the little version of the
original poison?

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:00.920
That's cuuute."

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:05.279
LiL Poison began competing professionally
at four, when he participated in a local Halo

00:04:05.279 --> 00:04:06.889
tournament in New York.

00:04:06.889 --> 00:04:11.640
Two years later, MLG signed the aforementioned
contract with him — but he only cemented

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:16.820
his status as a professional gamer when he
placed second in MLG's free-for-all Halo tournament

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:18.459
in 2005.

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:23.110
Although professional video gamers keep getting
younger and younger, Lil Poison's record seems

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:25.960
like it'll be awfully hard to break.

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:27.970
Oldest video game record holder

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:32.560
At the other end of the spectrum from Lil
Poison sits John S. Bates, the oldest video

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.750
gamer in the world to hold a Guinness World
Record.

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:40.650
Starting in April 2008, Bates started recording
every perfect game with a 300 point score

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:45.380
that he played in Wii Sports Bowling at his
home in Onalaska, Wisconsin.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:50.430
By June 2012, Bates, who was 85 years old
at the time, had amassed a record-setting

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.940
14,000 perfect matches.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:55.280
But he didn't stop playing just because he
set a record.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:00.110
Over the next three years, Bates accumulated
another 6,000 perfect matches, bringing his

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:05.300
grand total to 20,000 perfect bowling matches
by April 2015.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:10.780
That's impressive, but come on… what else
are you going to do in Onalaska, Wisconsin?

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:13.150
World's largest video game collection

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:15.789
Most people buy video games to play them.

00:05:15.789 --> 00:05:20.590
But Australian-born Joel Hopkins collects
video games for the sake of collecting them.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:26.070
As of October 2015, Hopkins had amassed a
collection of 18,000 games.

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:30.360
It took Hopkins over 20 years to collect all
the games, but he really ramped up his hobby

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:35.110
in 2010, when he started actively searching
for older releases around the world.

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:40.160
Of course, collecting that many games — as
well as 200 consoles — cost a lot of money:

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.470
an estimated $2 million, to be exact.

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:48.740
Unless someone buys his collection, it's doubtful
a record like this will ever be broken.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:51.539
Beating Dark Souls using nine different controllers

00:05:51.539 --> 00:05:56.490
These days, it's no longer just about beating
a game; it's about how you beat it.

00:05:56.490 --> 00:06:00.860
We've seen a player beat Bloodborne using
a Rock Band guitar, but apparently that wasn't

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:06.210
enough for Canadian gamer Benjamin Gwin, who
used a Rock Band guitar, a Rock Band drum

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:11.250
kit, a Rock Band piano, a Donkey Kong bongo,
and an assortment of other controllers to

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:13.169
beat the notoriously difficult Dark Souls.

00:06:13.169 --> 00:06:14.169
Gwin earned the distinct Guinness World Record
for "most alternative control methods used

00:06:14.169 --> 00:06:17.110
to complete Dark Souls," which is Guinness
lingo for "he beat a super hard game with

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:20.750
ridiculous things, and we had to make up a
category for it."

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:22.970
And that's not to knock his accomplishment.

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:27.620
Gwin must have loved taking on this challenge,
because he plans to do it again — but with

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:33.069
an Xbox Kinect, a Power Glove, and a homemade
controller made out of bananas.

00:06:33.069 --> 00:06:38.160
It's not clear if that's insanity or dedication,
but we're rooting for him all the same.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:39.400
Thanks for watching!

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.510
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:44.889
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

